\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The retained cycle operator}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Let #{R} be any commutative ring, and let #{q\ge 2} and
#{N\ge 3} be integers. Write #{X=\mathbb Z/(qN)\mathbb Z}, and
let #{M=\mathcal F_R(X)} be the finite free site module of
functions from #{X} to #{R}, as in the
\vocabk{field modules}{fdip-0010}. Let #{T} be the canonical
shift endomorphism of #{M}, given by #{(Tf)(i)=f(i-1)}, with
indices read modulo #{qN}. For #{a,b\in R} set
##{Q=a\bbI -b(T+T^{-1})}
When #{2b} is a unit, the shape is #{t=a(2b)^{-1}}. Let #{K}
be the retained sites #{q\mathbb Z/(qN)\mathbb Z}, identified
with #{\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z} by #{qj\mapsto j}, and let #{Y}
be the complementary eliminated sites. Extension by zero and
restriction are the retained and eliminated inclusions and
projections #{\iota_K}, #{\iota_Y}, #{\pi_K} and #{\pi_Y}.
They form a \vocabk{chosen binary splitting}{fdip-001T} of
#{M}. The components are #{A=\pi_KQ\iota_K},
#{B=\pi_KQ\iota_Y}, #{E=\pi_YQ\iota_K} and
#{C=\pi_YQ\iota_Y}. Let #{T_r} and #{U_r} be the polynomials
of \ref{fdip-00B1}. Suppose #{2b} and #{U_{q-1}(t)} are
invertible. Then the \vocabk{split Schur map}{fdip-001T}
##{S=A-BC^{-1}E}
equals
##{S=\frac{b}{U_{q-1}(t)}\,P^K_{T_q(t)},}
where #{P^K_s=2s\,\bbI -(T_K+T_K^{-1})} is the
\vocabk{unit-coupling cycle operator}{fdip-00AW} formed on
the retained sites and #{T_K} is their canonical shift,
#{(T_Kf)(j)=f(j-1)}. The retained shape is #{T_q(t)} and
the retained coupling is #{b\,U_{q-1}(t)^{-1}}.
The eliminated set is #{N} disjoint paths of #{q-1} vertices.
The determinant of the compression #{C} is
#{(b^{q-1}U_{q-1}(t))^N}. These formulae use only the ring
operations and the two inverses, so they hold in any commutative
ring in which #{2b} and #{U_{q-1}(t)} are units.}

\p{Remark. In the counting basis of site indicators, the only
nonzero coordinates of #{Q} are #{Q_{ii}=a} and
#{Q_{i,i\pm 1}=-b}. When the unit hypotheses hold and
#{R=\mathbb R}, with the counting inner product, #{Q} is the
\vocabk{uniform cycle precision}{fdip-00AW}; for a Gaussian
law, #{S} is the precision of its decimation. The identities
above are not restricted to that specialization.}

\p{Remark. Suppose #{t=\cosh\theta} with #{\theta>0}, so
#{t>1}. The evaluation in \ref{fdip-00B1} sends one
decimation step by #{q} to the shape
#{T_q(t)=\cosh(q\theta)}, hence #{\theta\mapsto q\theta}.
Thus #{\theta} is the inverse correlation length in lattice
units, and that length scales by #{1/q}. The value #{t=1},
equivalently #{\theta=0}, is the massless fixed point that
every step preserves, because #{T_q(1)=1}. Every #{t>1}
moves away from it, because #{\cosh(q\theta)>\cosh\theta}.
The identity #{T_q\circ T_r=T_{qr}} is the semigroup law of
the steps. This reading uses the real evaluation. It is not
part of the ring calculation.}

\p{For #{q=2} the map on #{(a,b)} is
#{(a,b)\mapsto(a-2b^2/a,\,b^2/a)}. In \ref{fdip-0057} the
neighbor value is #{1} and the diagonal equals #{2} plus the
mass parameter. The map sends that precision to the retained
operator. The mass parameter there is the retained diagonal
minus twice the retained coupling.
Over an odd prime field, when #{\det C\neq 0}, the eliminated
sum is the factor #{Z_p(C)} in \ref{fdip-008O}. Let
#{r\ge 2} be an integer such that #{U_{r-1}(t)} and
#{U_{q-1}(T_r(t))} are units. A step by #{r} and then a step
by #{q} returns the retained coupling and the eliminated
determinant of the single step by #{qr}.}

\proof{
\p{Declare the counting basis of site indicators. Number the vertices #{0,\ldots,qN-1} and keep
#{0,q,\ldots,(N-1)q}. The #{N} paths of #{q-1} vertices between
consecutive kept vertices are disjoint, so #{C} is their direct
sum. Let #{\kappa_m} be the determinant of one #{m\times m}
tridiagonal block, with #{\kappa_0=1} and #{\kappa_1=a}. Then
#{\kappa_m=a\kappa_{m-1}-b^2\kappa_{m-2}}. With
#{t=a(2b)^{-1}}, the identity #{\kappa_m=b^m U_m(t)} holds for
#{m=0} and #{m=1}, and the recurrence of #{U_m} carries it
forward. Thus #{\det C=(b^{q-1}U_{q-1}(t))^N}.}

\p{No two kept vertices are adjacent, so the kept block is
#{a\bbI_N}. Let #{m=q-1} and let #{C_1y=e_m}. The path recurrence
gives #{y_k=y_1U_{k-1}(t)}. The last row is
#{-by_{m-1}+ay_m=1}, and it gives
#{y_1=1/(bU_m(t))}. The case #{m=1} is
#{U_1(t)=a/b}. The couplings at the two ends are #{-b}, so
the [split Schur map](fdip-001T) gives the off-diagonal entry
#{-b^2 y_1=-b/U_m(t)}.
Each kept vertex meets two paths. The endpoint entry of the
inverse is #{\kappa_{m-1}/\kappa_m=U_{m-1}(t)/(bU_m(t))}, so
one path subtracts #{bU_{q-2}(t)/U_{q-1}(t)}. Two paths give
the diagonal
##{a-2b\frac{U_{q-2}(t)}{U_{q-1}(t)}.}
\ref{fdip-00B1} gives
#{T_q(t)=tU_{q-1}(t)-U_{q-2}(t)}. Since #{2bt=a},
##{2\cdot\frac{b}{U_{q-1}(t)}\,T_q(t)
=a-2b\frac{U_{q-2}(t)}{U_{q-1}(t)}.}
In the declared counting basis, that diagonal and the neighbor
entries #{-b/U_{q-1}(t)} are the entries of
#{\frac{b}{U_{q-1}(t)}\,P^K_{T_q(t)}}.
Nothing in the arithmetic requires more than the two units.}

\p{For #{q=2}, #{U_1(t)=a/b}, hence the retained coupling is
#{b^2/a} and the retained diagonal is #{a-2b^2/a}.
Substituting the diagonal and the neighbor value of
\ref{fdip-0057} returns its retained operator. The retained
mass parameter in \ref{fdip-0057} equals that diagonal minus
twice that coupling. Over an odd prime field, the block
factorization of \ref{fdip-008O} is the eliminated sum once
#{\det C\neq 0}, with this #{S}. When #{U_{r-1}(t)} and
#{U_{q-1}(T_r(t))} are units, start from length #{qrN}. The
first step has retained coupling #{b/U_{r-1}(t)}, and
##{\det(C_r)\det(C_q)
=(b^{r-1}U_{r-1}(t))^{qN}
\biggl(\frac{b^{q-1}U_{q-1}(T_r(t))}{(U_{r-1}(t))^{q-1}}\biggr)^N
=(b^{qr-1}U_{r-1}(t)\,U_{q-1}(T_r(t)))^N.}
\ref{fdip-00B1} replaces the product of these two units by
#{U_{qr-1}(t)}, so
#{\det(C_r)\det(C_q)=(b^{qr-1}U_{qr-1}(t))^N} and the coupling
#{\bigl(b/U_{r-1}(t)\bigr)/U_{q-1}(T_r(t))} equals
#{b/U_{qr-1}(t)}.}
}
